---
// Armature docs: the Skin station, plus AutoSkin.
// COPY SOURCE: MrMiata workspace/2026-07-22_authoring-platform/DOCS-SITE-ARMATURE.md.
// AutoSkin's numbers are from the shipping code: a ~9 GB one-time component, NVIDIA GPU
// with 6 GB+ VRAM (8 GB recommended). Do not re-round them.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import ArmatureDocHeader from '../../../components/ArmatureDocHeader.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import '../../../styles/designed-doc.css';
import '../../../styles/armature-docs.css';
import stageSkin from '../../../assets/armature/stage-skin.png';

// THE PASS LIBRARY IS GONE (2026-09-01). `PASSES` listed Create new pass, Overwrite current and
// Compare; the saved-version library and the two-up compare were both retired with the
// multi-mesh work, so a character is skinned or it is not. The page said otherwise for a fortnight.
const REFINE = [
  ['Painting', 'with Add and Replace that mean what they say: the influence you paint reaches exactly the value you asked for, locked influences never move, and the remainder is taken proportionally from unlocked ones.'],
  ['Flood', 'applies the same operation to a whole selection. Grow and shrink the selection ring by ring with Shift + > and Shift + <.'],
  ['Hammer and Chisel', 'smooths weights toward the selection average, with falloff profiles for soft domes and hard peaks.'],
  ['The wiggle', 'lets you grab any joint in the Skin station and pose it to see deformation at a spot the exercises do not cover. Scrub the timeline and it snaps back. Your animation preview is never contaminated by a test pose, and undo pops the test pose first.'],
];
---
<DocsLayout
  section="armature"
  title="Skinning | Armature Docs"
  description="Skinning in Armature: Geodesic Voxel solves on any machine, SkinTokens runs the AI weights on your GPU, and painting, smoothing and typed weights refine the result."
>
  <article class="dd am">
    <a class="dd-breadcrumb" href="/docs/armature/">Armature docs</a>

    <ArmatureDocHeader
      eyebrow="ARMATURE &middot; STATION 3"
      title="SKINNING"
      summary="Two local engines generate your weights, generating again is cheap, and painting and smoothing take the result to production level."
    />

    <section class="am-section">
      <div class="am-split">
        <figure class="am-shot">
          <Image src={stageSkin} alt="The Skin station in Armature, showing generated weights on an armoured knight with the influence list and weight brush" widths={[720, 1440]} sizes="(min-width: 900px) 44vw, 92vw" />
        </figure>
        <div>
          <h2 class="dd-label">Two engines, one menu</h2>
          <p class="am-p">
            This station is where Artist + AI is most literal: the machine proposes weights,
            you judge them in motion, and you decide what happens next. Accept, regenerate,
            or take over by hand.
          </p>
          <p class="am-p">The machine's move first. Generate Skin offers two engines, both local:</p>
          <ul class="am-list">
            <li><b>Geodesic Voxel</b> solves in seconds on any machine. Distances travel through the character's body instead of straight lines, so fingers resting near a leg never steal leg weights, and rigid parts (armor plates, props) bind hard to their bone. Deterministic: the same character always gets the same weights. It is a working starting point on any hardware, not a rival to the AI tier.</li>
            <li><b>SkinTokens</b> is the AI engine, running on your own GPU, and the best weights Armature can produce.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="am-p">
            Each solve replaces the current skin, and one undo brings the previous weights back.
            Trying the other engine, or re-solving after you have moved a joint, costs you the
            wait and nothing else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="am-section">
      <h2 class="dd-label">Refinement tools</h2>
      <p class="am-p">
        Then your move. When a character deserves production-level weights, the hand tools
        take over.
      </p>
      <ul class="am-list">
        {REFINE.map(([k, v]) => <li><b>{k}</b> {v}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="am-p">
        Much of the craft here is knowing when to stop painting and go fix the skeleton. A
        shoulder that deforms badly is usually a shoulder in the wrong place, and a fresh solve
        is quick enough that finding out costs you a minute.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="am-section" id="autoskin">
      <h2 class="dd-label">AutoSkin: the AI engine, on your machine</h2>
      <p class="am-p">
        AutoSkin generates skin weights and can propose full skeletons. It runs entirely on your
        own GPU. Nothing about your characters is uploaded anywhere.
      </p>
      <p class="am-p">
        It is a <b>one-time optional download</b> of roughly 9 GB (the inference engine and its
        model weights), offered on first launch and always available later under Generate Skin.
        Armature installs it, proves it end to end with a real test rig, and turns the feature on.
        The download resumes where it left off if interrupted, and everything else in Armature
        works without it.
      </p>
      <p class="am-note">Requirements: an NVIDIA GPU with at least 6 GB of VRAM (8 GB recommended).</p>
      <p class="am-p">
        Not taking the download, or no NVIDIA GPU? <b>Geodesic Voxel ships built in</b>, so every
        machine can skin from the first minute, and the AI tier is one download away whenever you
        want it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="am-pager" aria-label="Section">
      <a href="/docs/armature/skeleton/">&larr; Skeleton</a>
      <a href="/docs/armature/export/">Export &rarr;</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
